\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A finite-range Ginsparg–Wilson symbol in two dimensions}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{In two dimensions with spacing #{a>0}, take the spin and internal
spaces and a compatible grading as in \ref{fdip-014E}. Put
##{\begin{aligned}
m(r)&=a^{-1}(1-\cos p_1\cos p_2),\\
f(r)&=a^{-1}(\sin p_1,\ \cos p_1\sin p_2).
\end{aligned}}
The squared length in the sphere relation, times #{a^2}, is
#{\cos^2p_1(\cos^2p_2+\sin^2p_2)+\sin^2p_1=1}. Hence the
blocks give a Ginsparg–Wilson operator. Each sine or cosine
uses forward and backward unit shifts on its own axis, by
\ref{fdip-000S}. Products in different coordinates join only
sites separated by at most one step in each coordinate.}

\p{Let #{N_1\ge6} and #{N_2\ge4} be even. Vanishing of the
first component forces #{p_1\in\{0,\pi\}}. Then #{\cos p_1}
is nonzero and #{p_2\in\{0,\pi\}}. All #{4} corners are sampled.
At a corner write #{c_j=\cos p_j},
#{s_j=\sin(\frac{2\pi}{N_j})>0} and
#{q=\cos(\frac{2\pi}{N_1})>0}. Factor the diagonal map
#{J=a^{-1}\operatorname{diag}(c_1s_1,c_1c_2s_2)}
from the values of #{f} at the neighbors. The remaining
vectors for the coordinate steps are #{\pm(1,0),\pm(0,1)}.
At the two diagonal neighbors they are #{\pm(1,q)}, since
#{\cos(p_1\pm\frac{2\pi}{N_1})=c_1q}. These are all the rays
of the incident triangles in the subdivision of \ref{fdip-0141}.}

\p{The positive diagonal lies strictly between the positive axes,
and its negative lies between the negative axes. All #{6} rays
are therefore in the same cyclic order as the original grid
steps. Each consecutive pair spans a nondegenerate triangle
cone and a generic direction lies in exactly one. Inserting
#{J} multiplies every orientation by #{\operatorname{sgn}\det J=c_2}.
The vertex degrees at #{(0,0)} and #{(\pi,\pi)} are consequently
#{+1} and #{-1}. Both masses vanish. At #{(0,\pi)} and
#{(\pi,0)} the degrees are #{-1} and #{+1} and the mass is
#{2a^{-1}}. Thus the two zero blocks have opposite local degrees.
At #{N_1=4}, #{q=0} and a diagonal ray coincides with an axis,
so the same star calculation would lose nondegeneracy.}
